---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { DATA_YEAR, occupations, states } from '../lib/salary';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About SalaryByCity"
  description="Why SalaryByCity exists: free, signup-free salary data for every U.S. state, built transparently on Bureau of Labor Statistics figures — with no lead selling and no paywalls."
>
  <article class="prose-page mx-auto max-w-3xl py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">About SalaryByCity</h1>

    <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      SalaryByCity answers one deceptively simple question: <strong>what does a job actually pay
      where you live?</strong> National salary averages are quoted everywhere, but they are close to
      useless for real decisions. A registered nurse weighing offers in Sacramento and Jackson is
      looking at a pay gap of more than $50,000 a year — and a cost-of-living gap that rewrites what
      those numbers mean. We built this site to put that state-level picture in one place, for free.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">What we publish</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      We currently cover {occupations.length} occupations across all {states.length} U.S. states —
      {occupations.length * states.length} occupation-and-state combinations. Every page shows the
      median salary, the full percentile range from entry-level to top earners, how the state's cost
      of living changes what the salary is worth, which cities and industries do the hiring, and how
      the state's tax rules affect take-home pay. All figures are grounded in the U.S. Bureau of
      Labor Statistics' Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics program — the federal
      government's own wage survey — and our <a href="/methodology/" class="text-blue-700 underline dark:text-blue-400">methodology page</a>
      explains exactly how each number is produced.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">What makes us different</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      Most salary websites are lead-generation businesses. They show you a number so they can sell
      your attention — or your contact details — to recruiters, schools, and lenders. SalaryByCity
      does none of that. <strong>We don't sell leads. We don't require an account. There is no
      paywall, no "unlock full report" button, and no email gate.</strong> Every figure we have is on
      the page, for everyone, the moment it loads. The site is supported by ordinary display
      advertising, which keeps our incentives simple: publish accurate, useful pages, and be
      transparent about where the data comes from.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Who this is for</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      Job seekers comparing offers across state lines. Workers preparing for a salary negotiation
      who want a defensible, government-sourced benchmark. Students choosing between career paths.
      People considering a relocation who need to know whether a "raise" survives the move. If a
      salary decision depends on <em>where</em>, this site is for you.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Honesty about limits</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      Statistics describe markets, not individuals. Your offer will depend on your experience,
      employer, and negotiation — our numbers are the {DATA_YEAR} market context, not a promise.
      Where the underlying federal data is suppressed or modeled, we say so plainly on the
      <a href="/methodology/" class="text-blue-700 underline dark:text-blue-400">methodology page</a>.
      Questions, corrections, or suggestions are always welcome via our
      <a href="/contact/" class="text-blue-700 underline dark:text-blue-400">contact page</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
